  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A powerful coastal storm will continue to \ndepart today, but with lingering impacts to the region.\n\nStrong low pressure continues to slowly slide northward away\nfrom the region, but our area will continue to feel its\ninfluence today and even to a lesser degree on Saturday. Clouds\nand showers continue crossing the region, and while certainly\nnot as heavy or numerous as yesterday, much of the area,\nespecially northwest of I-95, will get additional rainfall.\nTemperatures will be more uniform, cooler in the south and\nmilder in the north, as a gusty, but not as strong as yesterday,\nsouthwest wind takes hold across the region. We'll see some\ngusts up above 30 mph, but not expecting gusts on land to exceed\n45 mph, so no wind headlines are in effect. That said, given\nsaturated soils and weakened conditions of some trees from\nyesterday's beating, wouldn't be at all surprised with some\nsporadic additional tree falls and resultant impacts, but they\nshould be very sporadic. \n\nClouds and showers should dwindle tonight and Saturday looks to\nbe dry with more sun, though a gusty southwest wind will\ncontinue as high pressure slowly builds back in, if only for a\nday or so. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor to moderate tidal flooding will continue \ntoday.\n\nTides remain elevated, and while the strong easterly flow piling\nup the water against the overall coast has abated, in some areas\nthe south-southwest winds will tend to hold water in, such as\nacross the Chesapeake Bay, tidal Delaware River, and some back\nbays. Thus, we still have some moderate tidal flooding to get\nthrough, particularly this morning, and some coastal flood\nwarnings remain in place. Elsewhere, water levels should\ncontinue slowly falling, but likely still reach minor flood\nthresholds, so coastal flood advisories are in place for just\nabout everyone else near the coast who isn't under a warning.\nWater levels are forecast to continue (or resume) falling by\ntonight, so we should start seeing headlines drop for good by\nthen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Low pressure is expected to lift north and west\nof the area Sunday night into Monday bringing showers and gusty\nwinds. \n\nA new area of low pressure will be lifting north and west of the\nregion Sunday night through Monday. This looks to bring a round\nof showers through mainly for the overnight period Sunday night.\nShowers should be moving out of the area into Monday but gusty\nWNW winds will be developing in the system's wake with the\npotential for gusts over 40 mph.",
